<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Who writes here and why the blog is called Confidently Wrong"
	pubDate={new Date('2026-05-13')}
	lang="en"
	translationHref={`${BASE}/pt/about`}
>

<p>I started coding at age 9. No YouTube. No Stack Overflow. No responsible adult nearby to warn me this would consume the next 40 years of my life.</p>

<p>I'm Sandro Bihaiko, born in Curitiba in 1974 — the same Curitiba with the bi-articulated buses and tube stations from the first post on this blog. I grew up thinking programming was as natural as riding a bike, until I realized most people don't spend weekends debugging code on computers that run hotter than a pizza oven.</p>

<p>Self-taught all the way. I started Electronic Engineering at CEFET, but my career pulled me in a different direction before the degree. Over more than two decades, I've held nearly every role this industry has to offer: developer, analyst, architect, consultant, CTO, and founder.</p>

<p>In the early 2000s, I created JBanana — an open source MVC framework older than Apache Struts, adopted by HSBC and ranked among the largest Brazilian open source projects of its time. I also wrote for Mundo Java magazine for three years and spoke at events like JustJava, TDC, and various open source conferences.</p>

<p>Along the way, I made games. One won SXSW Gamer's Voice Best Multiplayer. Others received awards at LEVEL UY as best independent game in Uruguay. I also built casino games exported to the US, Mexico, and Canada, plus Unity3D tools that reached the Top 10 of the Unity Asset Store.</p>

<p>At some point, I also built a digital whiteboard based on the Wii Remote that cost nearly 10 times less than traditional solutions — and worked better. The market responded in the most elegant way possible: flooding Brazil with even cheaper Chinese versions. Impeccable timing.</p>

<p>Today I live in Florianópolis, a city that triples in population every summer and turns any simple commute into an exercise in existential contemplation. The upside is plenty of time to think about systems, AI, and engineering while traffic slowly attempts to exist again.</p>

<p>Currently I'm founder of AIGHA, an AI and automation consultancy. Before that, I spent time at RD Station, where I consolidated over a decade of data into a datalake and helped build a team that — if I do say so myself — actually worked.</p>

<p>This blog is where I gather thoughts on technology, software, AI, and the organized chaos of building complex systems in an increasingly probabilistic era. I have strong opinions. Some turn out to be right. Others age terribly.</p>

<p>That's exactly why this blog is called <strong>Confidently Wrong</strong>.</p>

</Layout>
